Drop -Werror from AM_CFLAGS, for the gcc this chain builds with.

WHY: libtpms's configure.ac hard-codes -Werror into AM_CFLAGS, and the
stage-4 gcc defaults to gnu23, where glibc's <string.h> makes strstr()
return `const char *` for a `const char *` argument. Run 88777336449,
the first build of this package:

    tpm_library.c: In function 'TPMLIB_GetPlaintext':
    tpm_library.c:441:11: error: assignment discards 'const' qualifier
        from pointer target type [-Werror=discarded-qualifiers]
      441 |     start = strstr(stream, starttag);
    cc1: all warnings being treated as errors

The code is fine in C17 and warns in C23; -Werror turns a language
change into a build failure. The same ruling this chain already made
for the kernel (CONFIG_WERROR off: "a 2026 gcc warns about 2026 code")
and that Gentoo and Fedora make for this package and for swtpm.

WHY THE FLAG AND NOT THE LINE. Fixing tpm_library.c:441 alone fixes one
warning and leaves -Werror waiting for the next one -- gcc's own note in
the same log ("unrecognized command-line option '-Wno-self-assign'")
shows the flag set was written for clang and is already off this
compiler's map. The warnings stay visible in the log; they stop being
fatal.

WHERE: configure.ac, not the generated configure -- the recipe runs
autoreconf after unpack and the driver applies patches before that, so
this is the file that matters. One hunk, generated by diff -u against
the pinned tarball (sha256 edac0368...), not written by hand.

--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -349,7 +349,7 @@
 	AC_SUBST([HARDENING_LDFLAGS])
 fi
 
-AM_CFLAGS="$CFLAGS $COVERAGE_CFLAGS -Wall -Werror -Wshadow -Wreturn-type -Wsign-compare -Wno-self-assign -Wmissing-prototypes"
+AM_CFLAGS="$CFLAGS $COVERAGE_CFLAGS -Wall -Wshadow -Wreturn-type -Wsign-compare -Wno-self-assign -Wmissing-prototypes"
 AM_CFLAGS="$AM_CFLAGS"
 AM_LDFLAGS="$LDFLAGS $COVERAGE_LDFLAGS"
 
